INT. MINIVAN - PARKED AT AUTOMATIC CAR WASH - DAY

A cramped, crumb-strewn van with a cracked windshield and a bumper sticker reading "MY OTHER CAR IS ALSO THIS." Sunlight bounces off foam outside.

ANSELMO ROJAS, 40s, in a wrinkled polo, grips the wheel. HUI-MIN CHENG-ROJAS, 40s, in the passenger seat, juggles a grocery bag and a sippy cup. In the second row, ISAAC, 10, plays a handheld game, and NOOR, 7, hugs a stuffed giraffe. In the way back, GRANDMA FUMIKO, 80s, in a floral blouse, folds a paper crane. Strapped into a car seat beside her, baby TUCK, 1, chews a rubber pretzel.

ANSELMO
(to everyone)
Okay! Windows up! Doors locked! Nobody touch anything!

ISAAC
I'm not touching anything.

NOOR
I'm touching the giraffe.

HUI-MIN
Noor, that's allowed.

GRANDMA FUMIKO
(to Tuck)
Look, little one. The soap is coming.

TUCK
Ba!

The van rolls forward. Foam slaps the windshield. Brushes begin to thump along the sides.

ANSELMO
(muttering)
We're fine. We're fine. We're fine.

HUI-MIN
(softly, to him)
Anselmo, you have driven through a car wash before.

ANSELMO
Not with her.

He glances in the mirror at Grandma Fumiko, who folds another crane, serene.
